---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Sample = `let steps = [8200, 4500, 11000, 8200, 3000, 12500, 7600];
let goal = 8000;

function daysAtGoal(log, target) {
  let count = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < log.length; i++) {
    if (log[i] >= target) {
      count = count + 1;
    }
  }

  return count;
}

let hit = daysAtGoal(steps, goal);
console.log("You hit your goal on " + hit + " of " + steps.length + " days.");`;

const PromptC = `WRITTEN RESPONSE 2(c) — Data and Procedural Abstraction

You are given isEqual(value1, value2), which returns
true if the two values are equal and false otherwise.

Using the list in your Data segment, explain in
detailed steps an algorithm that counts the number of
elements equal to a certain value.

Your explanation must be detailed enough for someone
else to write the program code.`;

const ChecklistC = `THE FOUR THINGS THE ALGORITHM MUST DO

  1  go through EVERY element of the list
  2  call isEqual on each element
  3  keep a running count
  4  produce the count — return it, store it,
     or display it

Miss any one and the point is gone.`;

const WorkedC = `Using the list 'steps':

  1. Make a variable count and set it to 0.
  2. Look at each element of steps in turn, from the
     first to the last, until every element has been
     looked at.
  3. For each element, call isEqual with that element
     and the target value.
  4. If isEqual returns true, add 1 to count.
  5. After every element has been checked, count holds
     the number of elements equal to the target.
     Return count.`;

const PromptWR1 = `WRITTEN RESPONSE 1 — Program Design, Function, and Purpose

  • identify an expected USER of your program
  AND
  • describe one way the program's DESIGN meets that
    user's needs.

Both parts, and the second must connect to the first.`;

const WorkedWR1 = `An expected user is someone trying to build a daily
walking habit who wants to know whether they are
keeping it up.

The design meets that user's need because the program
counts how many days out of the week reached their
chosen goal and reports it as "X of 7 days" — so the
user sees at a glance whether they are on track,
without reading through every day's number themselves.`;

const BadWR1 = `ANSWERS THAT SCORE ZERO

  "The user is anyone who wants to track steps."
    — 'anyone' is not a user.

  "It's a helpful fitness app."
    — names no need and no design feature.

  "The user is a runner. The design uses a loop."
    — a user and a design feature, but the design
      is not connected to the RUNNER'S need.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 4.2: Written Responses 2(c) and 1</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Other Two Points</h2>

          <p>
            <b>The remaining two written responses.</b> WR2(c) is an algorithm you can
            walk in already knowing how to write &mdash; it is the same shape every
            year. WR1 is about who your program is for.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">WR2(c) is the most predictable question on the
            exam.</b> Same algorithm, over whatever list you happen to have put in your
            reference. You rehearsed it at <code>wr-algorithm</code>. Today you write
            the version you will hand in.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 4.2</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Written Responses 2(c) and 1</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>WR2(c) Is Almost Free</h2>
              <p>It is the same algorithm every year: <b>count how many elements equal a value.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">You are handed <code>isEqual</code>. You supply the loop and the count.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Learn it once now and it is a guaranteed point in May.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Four Things, All Four Required</h2>
              <p>Go through <b>every</b> element.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Call <code>isEqual</code> on each.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Keep a running <b>count</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Produce it &mdash; return, store, or display.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>"Detailed Enough for Someone Else to Code"</h2>
              <p>That is the phrase in the prompt, and it is the bar.</p>
              <p class="fragment">&quot;Count how many match&quot; is true and scores nothing.</p>
              <p class="fragment">It has to be steps somebody could follow without guessing.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>WR1: Name a Person and a Need</h2>
              <p>An <b>expected user</b> &mdash; a specific someone, not &quot;anyone&quot;.</p>
              <p class="fragment">And one way the <b>design</b> meets <b>that</b> user's need.</p>
              <p class="fragment">The two halves have to connect.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The WR1 Trap</h2>
              <p>A user, and a design feature, that have nothing to do with each other.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><i>&quot;The user is a runner. The design uses a loop.&quot;</i></p>
              <p class="fragment">True, true, and zero &mdash; the loop does not meet the runner's need.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Writing the WR2(c) counting algorithm against your own list, all four requirements</li>
                <li>Making the description detailed enough to code from</li>
                <li>Identifying a specific expected user of your program</li>
                <li>Connecting one design feature to that user's need</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Worked: WR2(c) -->
        <section id="wr2c">
          <h2>Worked Example &mdash; WR2(c)</h2>

          <p>The same fictional program:</p>
          <Code code={Sample} frame="code" lang="javascript" class="mb-4" />

          <Code code={PromptC} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Prompt" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-4" />

          <Code code={ChecklistC} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Four Requirements" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-4" />

          <p>A full-credit answer:</p>
          <Code code={WorkedC} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Detailed Enough to Code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <h5 class="mt-4">Check it against the four</h5>
          <ul>
            <li><b>Every element</b> &mdash; &quot;from the first to the last, until every element has been looked at.&quot;</li>
            <li><b>Calls <code>isEqual</code></b> &mdash; on each element and the target.</li>
            <li><b>Running count</b> &mdash; a variable that starts at 0 and goes up.</li>
            <li><b>Produces it</b> &mdash; returns <code>count</code>.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The clause students drop is &quot;until every
            element has been looked at.&quot;</b> Without it, the description could stop
            at the first match &mdash; and a reader could not tell whether you meant to
            count or just to find.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Worked: WR1 -->
        <section id="wr1">
          <h2>Worked Example &mdash; WR1</h2>

          <Code code={PromptWR1} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Prompt" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-4" />

          <p>A full-credit answer:</p>
          <Code code={WorkedWR1} frame="code" lang="txt" title="User, and Need Met" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-4" />

          <p>And the ones that do not earn it:</p>
          <Code code={BadWR1} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Three Ways to Score Zero" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>The third bad answer is the instructive one.</b> It has a real user and
            a real design feature &mdash; and it still scores zero, because the design
            feature is not described as meeting <b>that user's</b> need. <b>The two
            halves have to connect.</b>
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Apply -->
        <section id="apply">
          <h2>Now Your Own Program</h2>

          <p><b>Open your Project Reference.</b> Both responses, in full sentences, on paper.</p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Your WR2(c)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Name the list in your Data segment.</li>
            <li>Pick a value to count &mdash; any value the list could hold.</li>
            <li>Write the algorithm in numbered steps, and check it against all four requirements yourself.</li>
            <li><b>The clause to include:</b> that the algorithm looks at every element, not just until it finds one.</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Your WR1</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Name <b>one specific expected user.</b> Not &quot;anyone&quot;, not &quot;people&quot; &mdash; a person with a situation.</li>
            <li>Describe <b>one feature of your design</b> and say <b>how it meets that user's need.</b></li>
            <li>Read it back: does the design half connect to the user half? If they are two separate facts, rewrite until they meet.</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Keep both answers.</b> The WR2(c) you write today
            is nearly the WR2(c) you write in May &mdash; only the list name and the
            value change. This is the cheapest exam point available to you.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Name the four things a WR2(c) algorithm must do.</li>
            <li>Why does &quot;count how many match&quot; score zero?</li>
            <li>Why is &quot;anyone who wants to track steps&quot; not a valid WR1 user?</li>
            <li>What makes the third bad WR1 answer fail, even though both its halves are real?</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Your WR2(c) and WR1, in full sentences, about your own program.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
